WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.940
Did you fall in love with Fleabag?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.550
Well, start getting used to life without her
endlessly witty remarks.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.640
"They're good, right?

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:08.900
Chic."

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.160
"'Chic' means 'boring.'

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.220
Don't tell the French."

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.580
Series mastermind Phoebe Waller-Bridge created,
wrote, and starred in the hit show, portraying

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.140
a young woman who uses sex, humor, and an
imaginary audience to cope with difficult

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:21.900
emotions.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.140
Well, Waller-Bridge just revealed that the
Emmy-winning show won't be getting a third

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.820
season at least not anytime soon.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.260
Please note: In case you aren't completely
caught up with Fleabag, there's going to be

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.760
some spoilers ahead.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.000
During an appearance on Late Night with Seth
Meyers, Waller-Bridge revealed that she has

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.260
no immediate plans to revisit Fleabag a character
she originally conceived on the fly for a

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.260
stand-up sketch.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:50.920
"Yeah, I quite like the idea of coming back
to her — well, me — when I'm 50 'cause

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.600
I feel like she would've had more life then,
and God knows what she would've got up to."

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.080
She goes on to say:

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.140
"Actually, seeing a character like that in
her later stage of life is exciting, but for

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.520
now, she's been through enough.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.020
We've got to let her go."

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:09.280
In fact, Waller-Bridge never even planned
for Fleabag to get a second season.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.040
In August 2019, she told Jimmy Fallon on The
Tonight Show that

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.920
"I was very smug about not coming back because
I thought I had a lot of artistic integrity

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.740
in not coming back after the first one.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.480
Because the first season had a proper ending
in my mind."

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.800
Based on Waller-Bridge's 2013 one-woman show,
Fleabag eventually did receive a fantastic

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.280
second season which centered around Fleabag
falling in love with Andrew Scott's "Hot Priest."

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.460
Since a second season wasn't originally in
the cards, it's easy to believe Waller-Bridge

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.300
will eventually change her mind about making
a third season.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.300
While that's certainly a possibility, it sounds
like it probably won't happen for a few decades

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.420
but really, it might be best if it doesn't
happen at all.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.260
It's rare to find a series as well-written,
funny, and moving as Fleabag so it's only

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:57.100
natural to want new episodes to come out every
year.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.720
That said, it's also rare that a series maintains
such a high level of quality throughout multiple

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:03.440
seasons.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.880
Both seasons of Fleabag were nothing short
of marvellous.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.100
The show was pitch-perfect and expertly executed
from the first scene to the last … a scene

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.580
that finds Fleabag and the Priest confessing
their love for one another.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.480
"No, let's just leave that out there just
for a second on its own."

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.760
Then, they say a tearful goodbye and split
up with smiles on their faces.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.550
This unexpectedly touching ending caps off
the unique love story of Fleabag and The Priest,

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.310
setting The Priest back on the right path,
and putting Fleabag on a path where she can

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:36.920
start to heal and eventually shake off her
self-destructive tendencies.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.300
This conclusion also leaves the story wide
open and on a surprisingly hopeful note.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.000
Fleabag and the Priest can hold the memory
of each other in their hearts, and the two

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.840
of them can realize their personal goals independently.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.680
In the first season, Fleabag finds numerous
ways to avoid dealing with her grief and trauma

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.160
… and she finally begins to confront these
issues in the second season.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:01.720
That made the series finale poignant in all
the right places, and it never got overly

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:02.560
soapy.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.480
Fleabag realizes that she needs to stand on
her own two legs without constantly checking

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.900
in with the audience that only she can see.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.580
As Vulture's Kathryn VanArendonk put it,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:17.060
"She ends the story she's been telling us
while also turning toward her own life, her

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.880
own family, her own relationships with people
who can speak back to her.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.000
It's why the ending is such a perfect conclusion
to the series, and why it also feels so crushing.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.420
In the end, Fleabag breaks up with us."

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:32.240
Breaking up is never easy to do... but sometimes,
it's necessary.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.120
And when it comes to Fleabag, it's definitely
for the best.

